[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon DISC RESOLVED: 2.340 deg radius (4.68 deg across, 9.0x the real moon's 0.26), gain 0.150. At 2200x1300 / 90 deg FOV that is about 90 pixels wide. Written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky (MoonAngularRadius + MoonBrightness), so the asset defaults are dead -- voxel.Sky.MoonAngularRadiusDeg and voxel.Sky.MoonDiscBrightness are the knobs. These move the DISC ONLY; the ground is lit by voxel.Sky.MoonIntensity.
[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon has SET: altitude -33.63 deg, azimuth 356.7 deg, 100% illuminated (phase 0.497, 0=new 0.5=full). MoonEnabled=1, disc radius 2.340 deg. A moon BELOW the horizon or at ~0% illuminated is not a bug -- both draw nothing on purpose.
[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky star map handedness RESOLVED: StarUDirection=+1.0 (read back from /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky, raw +1.000), so StarRotation is being driven as +1.0 * (LST/360 + 0.0000 turns). Flipping the MPC scalar mirrors the map AND re-signs this drive together; driving +LST against a -1 map would rotate the sky backwards.
[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky StarAmbientGain RESOLVED to 1.0000, DERIVED from kStarAmbientCalibration (voxel.Sky.StarAmbientGain requested -1.0000; written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky). Derived means appearance and illumination cannot drift apart unnoticed; the constant was measured at voxel.Sky.StarGain 0.15 against a snowfield whose ground/sky luminance ratio came out 0.790, i.e. snow's albedo. This is the SkyLight capture's star gain ONLY -- it cannot change a main-view pixel, because M_SkyAtmosphereDome routes it through a ReflectionCapturePassSwitch. It is the RATIO of capture star radiance to visible star radiance: voxel.Sky.StarGain (0.1500 here) is already inside the shared StarBrightness scalar both domes multiply, so this number carries none of it. 0 removes starlight from the ambient term entirely. Re-measure with tools/sky-albedo-check.py on a starlit capture: ground/sky luminance must equal the ground's albedo.
[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon colour RESOLVED: 5778 K illuminant (requested 12000 K, tint strength 0.00 against the sun's 5778 K), times the MEASURED lunar albedo (1.000, 0.975, 0.896) from T_MoonColor -- so the ground is lit by sunlight reflected off the same surface the disc is drawn with. Read back from the component: 5778 K. At tint strength 0 this is the SPECTRUM's answer, not a convention; voxel.Sky.MoonTintStrength 1 restores the old 12000 K blue and is the A/B -- see kMoonTemperatureK for why that argument is a real one.
[2026.08.16-23.40.09:083][  0]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky forward-shading PRIMARY is now the SUN (sun altitude +38.07 deg, moon altitude -33.63 deg, moon intensity 0.0000). Read back: sun priority 1, moon priority 0 -- these MUST differ or the editor's 'multiple directional lights are competing' warning returns (LightGridInjection.cpp:1662). This is the ONE light that lights translucency, water and volumetric fog; handover at sun -6.0 deg with 1 deg of hysteresis, derived from kMoonSunSuppressStartDeg.
